# SRG-APP-000163-AU-002470  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must disable accounts (individuals, groups, roles,
# and devices) after 35 days of inactivity.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000163-AU-002470
group:           V-206466
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-003627
control_rev5:    AC-2

contribution:    Splunk Enterprise has no inactivity timer of its own over the native
                 account store, but it does hold the inbound provisioning path by which an
                 identity provider that detects 35 days of inactivity applies the
                 deactivation to the account this instance holds: splunkd launches the
                 supervisor process that handles SCIM authorization tokens, and Splunk Web
                 exposes the automated user management controls that configure it. Keeping
                 that path available is the application's share.

governs:         server.conf - Core server settings, including the certificates and protocols used to
                 protect management and replication traffic.
governs:         web-features.conf - Which features of the web interface are available.

check:           server.conf [teleport_supervisor] disabled in 0|false
check:           web-features.conf [feature:identity_sidecar_scim] enabled in 1|true

external:        The 35-day inactivity determination is made by the organization's
                 directory service or SAML identity provider, which must then apply the
                 deactivation to this instance over SCIM; that component is assessed under
                 its own baseline.
external:        The native account store exposes no inactivity timer.
                 authentication.conf.spec:487 expirePasswordDays and :504
                 expireUserAccounts govern password expiry, not account inactivity, and the
                 lockout settings act on consecutive failed logons, not on inactivity, so
                 where a site relies on native local accounts the requirement is carried by
                 an organizational account-review procedure. No Splunk setting tracks the
                 last successful logon per account, so the 35-day determination itself
                 cannot be read from Splunk configuration.
external:        The account of last resort that the fix text exempts is identified by the
                 organization; its role-scoped counterparts are authorize.conf.spec:867
                 [capability::never_lockout] and :870 [capability::never_expire].

citation:        ADDED, not corrected: this requirement is present in
                 inventory/groups/AC-1.json as the seventeenth member of the group and
                 carries an APPLICABLE determination in inventory/sca-determinations.json,
                 but no criterion was derived for it, so the row would stay not_reviewed
                 with nothing recorded about what the application can and cannot do. The
                 criterion follows the two settings the determination names and that
                 inventory/checks.tsv already carries as declared keys. Both are
                 deliberately written against their vendor defaults, which are the
                 permissive state: server.conf.spec:6591 gives disabled a default of false
                 and web-features.conf.spec:409 gives enabled a default of true, so the
                 criterion passes an instance that has left the provisioning path intact
                 and fails only one that has explicitly turned it off. Written the other
                 way round, as a demand that an organization adopt SCIM provisioning, it
                 would be over-reach, since a site using only native local accounts has no
                 Splunk setting that disables an account for inactivity and would be failed
                 for a lever the product does not provide. The third key in the inventory
                 row, authentication.conf scimSupportedDomains, is excluded on two grounds:
                 checks.tsv marks it undeclared, and it is absent from the specification
                 set provided here, so no vocabulary or default can be cited for it.
                 Caution on citations generally, from the same determination: the provided
                 specification snapshot is shorter than the set 10.4.2 ships and the line
                 numbers diverge, [teleport_supervisor] sitting at snapshot line 6584
                 against shipped line 6998 and [splunk_auth] at snapshot 437 against
                 shipped 458. Every file:line in this review resolves against the snapshot
                 supplied as the input, and any criterion republished against a shipped
                 instance must have its citations re-resolved.
                 This requirement is decidable: machine, so it carries no manual_reason:
                 that field is read only for a manual requirement. What the application
                 controls is the inbound provisioning path, which the two conditions decide
                 and which an administrator can turn off. What it does not control, the
                 35-day inactivity determination and the absence of any native
                 last-logon or inactivity setting, is named in the external dependencies
                 above and is not counted against the application.
finding:         Splunk Enterprise has disabled the SCIM provisioning path through which an
                 identity provider applies an inactivity-triggered deactivation, so the
                 application has foreclosed the only mechanism by which it can contribute
                 to disabling accounts after 35 days of inactivity; the inactivity
                 determination itself rests with the identity provider and, for native
                 local accounts, with an organizational account-review procedure.
